1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,940
The next section that we want to cover is cell death, and basically the idea, before we actually jump into the topic, the idea to keep in mind is, so far we've talked about stress, and we said that when a cell undergoes, when a cell or an organ is placed under stress, it may undergo growth adaptations.

2
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:30,940
So, for example, an increase in stress results in hyperplasia and hypertrophy, as we've already covered. A decrease in stress would result in atrophy. A change in stress would result in metaplasia.

3
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:42,940
If the stress is severe, or if the cell doesn't have the ability to adapt, then injury will occur, and the initial stage of injury is reversible. Basically, it's called reversible cellular injury.

4
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:52,940
And if the injury persists, the reversible injury can actually become irreversible. And that irreversible injury is basically, is also known as cell death.

5
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:58,940
And so, that's really where we are in our global, sort of, grand scheme view of things.

6
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:05,940
Now, when we talk about cell death, the morphologic hallmark of cell death is important to be familiar with.

7
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:13,940
And that, basically, is the loss of the nucleus. I.e., if I'm looking at a slide, and I'm looking at cells on a slide,

8
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:19,940
if I see that the cells no longer have their nuclei, that indicates that the tissue is now dead.

9
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:25,940
So, the morphologic hallmark of cell death is loss of the nucleus. It's not enough to know that.

10
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:33,940
The examiners like to go a step further, and they like you to know the mechanism by which the nucleus is lost.

11
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:38,940
And the mechanism by which the nucleus is lost, the nucleus is lost, is basically threefold.

12
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:45,940
First, the nucleus shrinks down. And that's called pycnosis. Actually, pycnosis, the word pycnosis means ink dot.

13
00:01:45,940 --> 00:01:52,940
And the nucleus basically shrinks down, like a, into a small little ink dot.

14
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:57,940
After shrinking down, the next stage is breaking up of the nucleus, and that's called karyorexis,

15
00:01:57,940 --> 00:02:02,940
where the nucleus breaks up into big pieces. And finally, the final stage is karyolysis,

16
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:07,940
where those pieces are then actually broken down into basic building blocks.

17
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:15,940
And so, the three phases of loss of the nucleus are karyorexis, sorry, pycnosis, karyorexis, and karyolysis.

18
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:19,940
Very important to be familiar with, relatively high yield terms.

19
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:26,940
Now, there are two mechanisms of cell death. The first is, and we have to be familiar with both,

20
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:31,940
the first is necrosis, and the second is apoptosis. And the way I kind of think about these is,

21
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:39,940
necrosis is sort of murder. Something comes from the outside, or some pathologic process from the outside occurs,

22
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:46,940
which then causes a grand scale destruction of the tissue, and that's necrosis.

23
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:51,940
And apoptosis, on the other hand, sort of the way I think about it, it's cellular suicide.

24
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:56,940
The cells independently sort of decide to remove themselves from the organ.

25
00:02:56,940 --> 00:03:04,940
Usually involves single cells or small groups of cells, and often there is no extensive tissue reaction to apoptosis.

26
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:10,940
These principles will be highlighted as we move forward in this presentation.

27
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:17,940
So, necrosis. Again, necrosis is death of a large group of cells. That's important.

28
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:21,940
And very important is that it is followed by acute inflammation.

29
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:26,940
This is going to come into play later as well when we cover inflammation,

30
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:29,940
but I'd like to actually stop here and stress this point.

31
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:35,940
There are two things, or there are two processes that result in acute inflammation.

32
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:40,940
The first, and the most commonly sort of thought about, is when you get an infection.

33
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:43,940
If you get a bacterial infection, you're obviously going to get acute inflammation.

34
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:48,940
But the second driving factor for acute inflammation is actually necrosis.

35
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:51,940
So, for example, when patients get a myocardial infarction,

36
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:55,940
one of the early changes that occurs is that neutrophils come into the tissue.

37
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:59,940
And another important change is that the neutrophil count goes up in the blood

38
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:03,940
because neutrophils are being generated in order to be sent to the tissue.

39
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:10,940
So, necrosis, I would say, is very important to remember that it is followed by acute inflammation.

40
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:16,940
Now, necrosis is always pathologic. It's always due to some pathologic process.

41
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:21,940
And it's never physiologic, meaning we don't have necrosis occurring as part of our normal physiology.

42
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:24,940
It always means that there's some disease or problem present.

43
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:28,940
And it can be divided into several types based on gross features.

44
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:35,940
These are high yield. Often, these questions often show up on examinations concerning the subtypes of necrosis.

45
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:40,940
And so we're going to actually spend a good degree of time on this, on the subtypes of necrosis.

46
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:45,940
The first type of necrosis is called coagulative necrosis.

47
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:50,940
What is coagulative necrosis? It is necrotic tissue that remains firm.

48
00:04:50,940 --> 00:05:02,940
I.e., the tissue sort of coagulates, or why don't we say the cellular proteins actually coagulate so that the cells retain their shape and the organ structure is preserved.

49
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:08,940
So, otherwise stated, the tissue dies. However, the general structure of the tissue remains.

50
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:16,940
And if we were to look at the tissue on a microscopic slide, we would see that the cells are present and the architecture of the cells is present.

51
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:20,940
However, the nuclei have disappeared. And so that's what we say here.

52
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:27,940
It's the tissue remains firm. There's coagulation of cellular proteins. However, the nucleus disappears.

53
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:35,940
The characteristic circumstance in which coagulative necrosis occurs is ischemic infarction.

54
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:41,940
Every organ in the body that undergoes ischemic infarction will actually exhibit coagulative necrosis.

55
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:51,940
The only exception is the brain. So, again, coagulative necrosis is seen with ischemia of any organ except the brain.

56
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:57,940
Here's an image from your text, figure 1.5, that just highlights what we mean by coagulative necrosis.

57
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:00,940
Now, let's start with this image here on the right.

58
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:05,940
This is the glomerulus, what I'm circling, and these are the tubules.

59
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:10,940
And you can clearly see that the glomerulus contains nuclei within the cells,

60
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:14,940
and that the tubules also contain nuclei within the cells, and that's normal.

61
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:19,940
I just put this normal picture here to establish a baseline in your mind.

62
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:22,940
Now, let's look here on the left at the necrosis.

63
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:23,940
Here is the glomerulus.

64
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:27,940
You still recognize that it looks sort of like a glomerulus.

65
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:30,940
However, there are no nuclei within the cells.

66
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:32,940
Here are the tubules.

67
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:34,940
Still recognize that these are tubules.

68
00:06:34,940 --> 00:06:38,940
However, there are no nuclei, no blue nuclei, within these cells.

69
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:45,940
And so that, then, this histologic picture is characteristic of coagulative necrosis.

70
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:46,940
All right.

71
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:50,940
A couple important derivative points regarding coagulative necrosis.

72
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:54,940
Usually, the area of infarcted tissue is wedge-shaped and pale.

73
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:55,940
All right.

74
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:59,940
Usually, the area of infarcted tissue is wedge-shaped and pale.

75
00:06:59,940 --> 00:07:01,940
This is not so hard to remember.

76
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:06,940
Basically, this is based on a principle, an anatomic principle, and that is that,

77
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:10,940
generally speaking, when vessels enter into an organ and feed an organ,

78
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:14,940
they dichotomously branch as they go through the organ.

79
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:17,940
So, let's say that there is a vessel coming this way,

80
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:20,940
and then there's a branch point here, and then another branch point here,

81
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:23,940
as the artery branches to feed the organ.

82
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:29,940
If you cut the blood supply here, everything from this vessel and everything from this vessel

83
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:34,940
is going to die, and it's going to create sort of everything in the distribution of this area

84
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:39,940
will become, you know, like a wedge-shaped, notice that this looks like a wedge,

85
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:42,940
a wedge-shaped area of necrosis.

86
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:50,940
And one of the classic features to be aware of is that the wedge points to the area of occlusion,

87
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:57,940
and that's because it's pointing to the area where the dichotomous branching was occurring.

88
00:07:57,940 --> 00:08:01,940
So, I don't think it's hard to remember that these areas are usually wedge-shaped.

89
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:03,940
Why are they pale?

90
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:04,940
Well, same principle.

91
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:07,940
I mean, if this is the blood supply coming in, and this is the blood supply branching,

92
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:09,940
then you cut the blood supply.

93
00:08:09,940 --> 00:08:12,940
Once you cut the blood supply, this tissue is going to die.

94
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:16,940
And once it dies, blood will obviously not be able to come back into it,

95
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:18,940
so the dead tissue will look pale.

96
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:21,940
No rocket science. Pretty straightforward.

97
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:26,940
Here's a picture from your text, figure 1.5, and it's basically highlighting that.

98
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:33,940
This is the cortex of the kidney, and this area here represents the area of infarction.

99
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:39,940
There was probably an occlusion here, and this tissue within the area of the triangle has died.

100
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:44,940
Now, you can partly recognize by the fact that there is indentation of the tissue here,

101
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:49,940
but also, it's a little subtle, but there's disruption of the normal tissue in this region.

102
00:08:49,940 --> 00:08:55,940
And so, this is a classic example. It's relatively pale, and it's kind of wedge-shaped.

103
00:08:55,940 --> 00:08:58,940
You know, it gives you an idea of what we're referring to.

104
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:05,940
Another important principle, or derivative principle, is that we could get red infarction within a tissue.

105
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:09,940
And red infarction requires two things in order for it to be seen.

106
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:12,940
First, blood must re-enter the tissue.

107
00:09:12,940 --> 00:09:17,940
And second, the tissue must be loosely organized so that it can hold the blood,

108
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:19,940
giving it a red color.

109
00:09:19,940 --> 00:09:21,940
So, these two things are required.

110
00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:25,940
Blood must re-enter the tissue, and the tissue must be loosely organized.

111
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:29,940
A classic example is shown here in your text, and this is figure 1.6.

112
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:33,940
This is a testicle, and this testicle has undergone hemorrhagic infarction.

113
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:38,940
Basically, the idea here is that this testicle is completely red.

114
00:09:38,940 --> 00:09:48,940
Now, in order to understand this, we probably should take a step back and remind ourselves that coming in this area here,

115
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:52,940
maybe I should create a blank screen to make it more easy to see.

116
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:57,940
When we've got, when we think about the testicle, coming into the testicle, you've got this cord,

117
00:09:57,940 --> 00:10:02,940
which basically the testicle hangs on, and then this is the testicle here.

118
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:09,940
Now, in this region, there is a thick-walled artery, and there is a thin-walled vein.

119
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:15,940
The classic mechanism by which testicular infarction occurs is that the testicle twists on this cord,

120
00:10:15,940 --> 00:10:22,940
and when it twists on the cord, the artery continues to allow blood to flow in because it's thick-walled.

121
00:10:22,940 --> 00:10:28,940
However, the vein collapses, and when the vein collapses, blood comes in, but blood can't go out,

122
00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:36,940
and if you recall from our lecture concerning ischemia, one of the ways by which you can get ischemia is blocking of the venous supply

123
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:39,940
because fresh blood would not be then passing across the organ.

124
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:44,940
So the organ will die because the vein, or the tissue will die because the vein is blocked,

125
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:50,940
and blood will continue to re-enter because the artery remains somewhat open relative to the blockage of the vein.

126
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:57,940
As blood piles up within the testicle, the testicle is relatively loose, the tissue of the testicle is relatively loose,

127
00:10:57,940 --> 00:11:04,940
and that will then create what we call a red infarction, and it is important for examinations to be familiar with

128
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:09,940
the distinction between a pale infarction and a red infarction.

129
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:11,940
Okay, so let's continue on.

130
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:16,940
The next type of necrosis is liquefactive necrosis, and guess what?

131
00:11:16,940 --> 00:11:19,940
In liquefactive necrosis, the tissue becomes liquefied.

132
00:11:19,940 --> 00:11:24,940
So we've got necrotic tissue, or dead tissue, that becomes liquefied.

133
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:31,940
Now, the way by which the tissue becomes liquefied is that there is enzymatic lysis of the cells.

134
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:40,940
The cells get acted on by some enzyme, and that is what basically destroys the tissue, resulting in the liquefaction.

135
00:11:40,940 --> 00:11:44,940
It is seen characteristically in three circumstances.

136
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:46,940
The first is brain infarction.

137
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:50,940
Recall that I told you that when you cut the blood supply to any organ in the body,

138
00:11:50,940 --> 00:11:52,940
you actually get coagulative necrosis.

139
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:58,940
However, if the blood supply is cut to the brain, that would result in liquefactive necrosis.

140
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:04,940
So that would be one important circumstance that results in liquefactive necrosis.

141
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:06,940
One of the questions we can ask ourselves is,

142
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:12,940
why is it that within the brain we get liquefactive necrosis, but in all other tissues we get coagulative necrosis?

143
00:12:12,940 --> 00:12:19,940
And the answer is because the brain contains microglial cells, which are derivatives of monocytes.

144
00:12:19,940 --> 00:12:22,940
They're sort of the macrophages of the brain.

145
00:12:22,940 --> 00:12:31,940
And the microglial cells contain hydrolytic enzymes that trash the tissue, or destroy the tissue, after it dies.

146
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:37,940
And so this is mediated, this brain, this circumstance here is mediated by microglial cells.

147
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:40,940
And that's actually an important principle as well.

148
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:45,940
The second circumstance in which we might see liquefactive necrosis is an abscess.

149
00:12:45,940 --> 00:12:48,940
An abscess is a walled off area of dead tissue.

150
00:12:48,940 --> 00:12:53,940
And it becomes liquefied because the neutrophils contain hydrolytic enzymes.

151
00:12:53,940 --> 00:12:58,940
And those hydrolytic enzymes, they destroy the tissue, resulting in the liquefaction of that tissue.

152
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:03,940
And so if I asked you what mediates liquefactive necrosis in the case of an abscess,

153
00:13:03,940 --> 00:13:06,940
the answer is the enzymes from the neutrophils.

154
00:13:06,940 --> 00:13:11,940
The third circumstance that we might see liquefactive necrosis is in pancreatitis.

155
00:13:11,940 --> 00:13:12,940
What is pancreatitis?

156
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:20,940
Remember that pancreatitis is a disease process in which we activate the enzymes of the pancreas within the pancreas itself.

157
00:13:20,940 --> 00:13:25,940
Now normally the enzymes of the pancreas would obviously get activated in the gut and digest food.

158
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:30,940
However, if the enzymes of the pancreas get activated within the pancreatic parenchyma itself,

159
00:13:30,940 --> 00:13:33,940
the pancreatic enzymes will digest the pancreas.

160
00:13:33,940 --> 00:13:40,940
And it's the digestion of the pancreas that results in a liquefactive necrosis of the pancreas itself.

161
00:13:40,940 --> 00:13:47,940
Now I keep emphasizing this pancreas itself because in pancreatitis we also see another type of necrosis of the surrounding fat,

162
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:50,940
the fat that surrounds the pancreas, but that's called fat necrosis.

163
00:13:50,940 --> 00:13:52,940
And we'll talk about that in a minute.

164
00:13:52,940 --> 00:13:56,940
So actually pancreatitis kind of has two types of necrosis associated with it,

165
00:13:56,940 --> 00:13:59,940
both liquefactive of the pancreatic parenchyma,

166
00:13:59,940 --> 00:14:02,940
and fat necrosis of the peripancreatic fat.

167
00:14:02,940 --> 00:14:09,940
And if I asked you what's the mechanism on an examination of liquefactive necrosis and pancreatitis,

168
00:14:09,940 --> 00:14:15,940
the answer is that it's the enzymes from the pancreas that are digesting the pancreatic parenchyma.

169
00:14:15,940 --> 00:14:20,940
Another type of necrosis to be familiar with is gangrenous necrosis.

170
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:27,940
This is basically coagulative necrosis, but when you look at the tissue grossly it resembles mummified tissue.

171
00:14:27,940 --> 00:14:31,940
Basically that is really what we call gangrenous necrosis.

172
00:14:31,940 --> 00:14:35,940
Now this is characteristic of ischemia of the lower limb.

173
00:14:35,940 --> 00:14:37,940
That's really the highlight I think here.

174
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:44,940
You can also see it in the GI tract, but it's really I think the classic is ischemia of the lower limb.

175
00:14:44,940 --> 00:14:48,940
It's not uncommonly seen, particularly in diabetics.

176
00:14:48,940 --> 00:14:56,940
Diabetics they get occlusion or atheroscler, not occlusion, but I should say partial occlusion of the popliteal artery

177
00:14:56,940 --> 00:15:03,940
and due to atherosclerosis and that can result in decreased blood flow to the lower extremity,

178
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:07,940
which then subsequently can cause gangrenous necrosis of that lower extremity.

179
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:19,940
If the dead tissue becomes infected, that infected tissue can then undergo liquefactive necrosis and that can,

180
00:15:19,940 --> 00:15:24,940
that also can be a component of gangrenous necrosis, but in that case we call it wet gangrene.

181
00:15:24,940 --> 00:15:29,940
The wet being the pus and the inflammatory exudate present due to the superimposed infection.

182
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:32,940
Here's a figure from your text just highlighting this.

183
00:15:32,940 --> 00:15:37,940
Basically this is a foot, I think this is of a diabetic patient,

184
00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:45,940
and it is just showing you the mummification or the gangrenous necrosis due to gangrenous necrosis of the extremity.

185
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:50,940
And here it's a little more liquefactive where it looks like there's some superimposed infection,

186
00:15:50,940 --> 00:15:57,940
which has resulted in focal liquefactive necrosis of that tissue.

187
00:15:57,940 --> 00:15:59,940
Sorry, tripping on my words.

188
00:15:59,940 --> 00:16:04,940
Okay, caseus necrosis. This is another type of necrosis.

189
00:16:04,940 --> 00:16:12,940
It's characterized as a soft, friable, necrotic tissue with a quote-unquote cottage cheese-like appearance.

190
00:16:12,940 --> 00:16:15,940
I'm sure you've heard that term before.

191
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:19,940
The way I think about this, it's a little strange, but this is the way I think about it.

192
00:16:19,940 --> 00:16:23,940
It's basically liquefactive necrosis with something mixed in.

193
00:16:23,940 --> 00:16:26,940
And the reason I think about it that way, it kind of goes back to cooking.

194
00:16:26,940 --> 00:16:32,940
If you've ever made a gravy, you know that you take something very liquidy, you add some flour.

195
00:16:32,940 --> 00:16:35,940
When you mix in the flour, that sort of thickens up the soup.

196
00:16:35,940 --> 00:16:38,940
So in the same way, in caseus necrosis, you've got an infection.

197
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:40,940
It's creating liquefactive necrosis.

198
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:44,940
However, you throw in there something that thickens up the soup,

199
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:47,940
making it a little more cottage cheese-like in appearance.

200
00:16:47,940 --> 00:16:49,940
And so what thickens the soup?

201
00:16:49,940 --> 00:16:54,940
The wall of fungus, i.e. fungal infection, or mycobacterium, like TB.

202
00:16:54,940 --> 00:16:57,940
So this is really what results in caseus necrosis.

203
00:16:57,940 --> 00:17:02,940
It's characteristic of TB and fungal infection, in particular the granulomatous inflammation

204
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:05,940
that's seen with fungus and TB.

205
00:17:05,940 --> 00:17:10,940
This is a classic example of caseus necrosis.

206
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:13,940
And I'm highlighted here, as I'm outlining here in red.

207
00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:17,940
This is the white cottage cheese appearance of caseus necrosis.

208
00:17:17,940 --> 00:17:21,940
Fat necrosis refers to necrosis of adipose tissue.

209
00:17:21,940 --> 00:17:24,940
It has a classic chalky white appearance.

210
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:30,940
The reason it picks up this chalky white appearance is because when fat dies, fatty acids are released.

211
00:17:30,940 --> 00:17:38,940
And the fatty acids then have the ability to bind with calcium, which is a process called saponification.

212
00:17:38,940 --> 00:17:42,940
An important term to be familiar with, saponification.

213
00:17:42,940 --> 00:17:46,940
Basically what happens here is that when calcium deposits on that dead fat,

214
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:49,940
it gives a white chalky appearance to the tissue.

215
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:51,940
Here's a classic example.

216
00:17:51,940 --> 00:17:53,940
This is a pancreas.

217
00:17:53,940 --> 00:17:58,940
This long extended organ is a pancreas, figure 1.9 from your text.

218
00:17:58,940 --> 00:18:02,940
And here we can see sort of these little white chalky deposits at the outer edge of this tissue,

219
00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:05,940
which is the fat surrounding the pancreas.

220
00:18:05,940 --> 00:18:09,940
And this would be a classic example of fat necrosis.

221
00:18:09,940 --> 00:18:13,940
Now let me take a step back and remind you, because sometimes students can get confused.

222
00:18:13,940 --> 00:18:19,940
In pancreatitis, where you get chewing up of the pancreas by pancreatic enzymes,

223
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:23,940
and you get liquefactive necrosis of the pancreas,

224
00:18:23,940 --> 00:18:27,940
and you get fat necrosis of the peripancreatic fat.

225
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:30,940
Again, so that's fat necrosis.

226
00:18:30,940 --> 00:18:35,940
Now, fat necrosis is classically seen in two regions or with two processes.

227
00:18:35,940 --> 00:18:39,940
The first is trauma to fat, and the highest yield here would be breast.

228
00:18:39,940 --> 00:18:41,940
Trauma to the breast.

229
00:18:41,940 --> 00:18:43,940
The breast is a relatively fatty organ.

230
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:46,940
It contains a lot of adipose tissue.

231
00:18:46,940 --> 00:18:48,940
Trauma to the breast.

232
00:18:48,940 --> 00:18:52,940
For example, if a woman is in a car accident,

233
00:18:52,940 --> 00:18:56,940
and the breast is crushed against the steering column or steering wheel,

234
00:18:56,940 --> 00:18:59,940
that can result in fat necrosis, which would release fat.

235
00:18:59,940 --> 00:19:02,940
Now that could result in destruction of the fat cells,

236
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:06,940
release of the fatty acids, and calcium binding those fatty acids,

237
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:09,940
resulting in the classic appearance of fat necrosis.

238
00:19:09,940 --> 00:19:15,940
So trauma to fat can release those fatty acids, resulting in fat necrosis.

239
00:19:15,940 --> 00:19:20,940
Probably it's important to also mention here that this really comes into play,

240
00:19:20,940 --> 00:19:24,940
particularly in the breast, because it can clinically present in two ways.

241
00:19:24,940 --> 00:19:29,940
One way by which fat necrosis can present, especially on exams, is as a mass.

242
00:19:29,940 --> 00:19:35,940
And basically when the fat necrosis occurs, there is often a giant cell reaction

243
00:19:35,940 --> 00:19:39,940
because of the inflammatory response to the fat.

244
00:19:39,940 --> 00:19:45,940
And so there can be a breast mass, a woman comes in, a biopsy is performed,

245
00:19:45,940 --> 00:19:54,940
and they might tell you on the exam that the biopsy shows giant cells, fat, and calcification.

246
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:57,940
And so you would think about fat necrosis then.

247
00:19:57,940 --> 00:20:02,940
It's also important to be aware of fat necrosis in the breast because the way by which,

248
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:09,940
sort of the, one of the ideas behind mammography is that we look for,

249
00:20:09,940 --> 00:20:13,940
one of the findings in mammography, I should say, is calcification.

250
00:20:13,940 --> 00:20:15,940
That's one of the abnormal findings.

251
00:20:15,940 --> 00:20:21,940
And the presence of calcification is sometimes an indicator of ductal carcinoma in situ.

252
00:20:21,940 --> 00:20:26,940
However, it can also be seen in benign circumstances like fat necrosis.

253
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:30,940
A woman does not necessarily have to give a trauma, sorry,

254
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:34,940
a woman does not necessarily have to give a history of trauma for fat necrosis.

255
00:20:34,940 --> 00:20:40,940
It could have happened in a circumstance where maybe the woman would not even have been aware of trauma.

256
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:47,940
For example, she's a softball player or plays a sport and happens to get hit with the ball on the chest,

257
00:20:47,940 --> 00:20:50,940
and that could result in fat necrosis of the breast.

258
00:20:50,940 --> 00:20:58,940
Another circumstance in which fat necrosis would be characteristic is the pancreatitis-mediated damage of peripancreatic fat.

259
00:20:58,940 --> 00:21:01,940
But I think I've really harped on that enough.

260
00:21:01,940 --> 00:21:07,940
Saponification, high yield term and concept, I've already mentioned it.

261
00:21:07,940 --> 00:21:14,940
When fat is damaged, fatty acids are released, whether that be by trauma or by lipase from pancreatitis,

262
00:21:14,940 --> 00:21:19,940
and that allows the fat to join with calcium, and that's called saponification.

263
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:24,940
And if you are interested in sort of a side point, this is the way by which soap is made as well.

264
00:21:24,940 --> 00:21:31,940
So the soap, the consumer soap that we purchase, is actually made by the similar process of saponification.

265
00:21:31,940 --> 00:21:40,940
Now what is sort of a derivative point, but it gives us an opportunity to touch on a high yield idea,

266
00:21:40,940 --> 00:21:47,940
is that saponification is an example of something called dystrophic calcification.

267
00:21:47,940 --> 00:21:52,940
So why don't I take a step back and just remind you that generally speaking,

268
00:21:52,940 --> 00:21:55,940
calcium does not deposit in tissues of the human body.

269
00:21:55,940 --> 00:22:03,940
When it deposits in tissues in the human body, there's really two underlying mechanisms by which that occurs.

270
00:22:03,940 --> 00:22:09,940
The first is dystrophic calcification, and the idea here is that there is a dead or dying tissue,

271
00:22:09,940 --> 00:22:14,940
and that dead or dying tissue becomes a nidus for the calcium to deposit.

272
00:22:14,940 --> 00:22:17,940
The serum calcium is normal, the serum phosphate is normal.

273
00:22:17,940 --> 00:22:22,940
However, calcium can deposit on the tissue because the tissue is dying.

274
00:22:22,940 --> 00:22:27,940
So one example of dystrophic calcification would be saponification, where you have dead fat,

275
00:22:27,940 --> 00:22:29,940
which then picks up the calcium.

276
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:34,940
Another example of dystrophic calcification, as long as we're on the topic, would be somoma bodies.

277
00:22:34,940 --> 00:22:40,940
So the somoma bodies that we're so used to hearing about in papillary carcinoma of the thyroid,

278
00:22:40,940 --> 00:22:44,940
and in meningiomas, and in papillary serous carcinoma of the ovary,

279
00:22:44,940 --> 00:22:47,940
all of those are examples of dystrophic calcification.

280
00:22:47,940 --> 00:22:51,940
The tumor cells outgrow their blood supply, they're growing rapidly, they die,

281
00:22:51,940 --> 00:22:55,940
and it's on top of those dying cells that calcification occurs.

282
00:22:55,940 --> 00:22:59,940
Again, in dystrophic calcification, the serum calcium is normal,

283
00:22:59,940 --> 00:23:02,940
and the serum phosphate is also normal.

284
00:23:02,940 --> 00:23:08,940
Dystrophic calcification can be contrasted with another process,

285
00:23:08,940 --> 00:23:11,940
which is called metastatic calcification.

286
00:23:11,940 --> 00:23:19,940
In metastatic calcification, the serum calcium or the serum phosphate, either one, is elevated,

287
00:23:19,940 --> 00:23:26,940
and the elevated serum calcium or elevated serum phosphate has the ability to force calcium into tissues,

288
00:23:26,940 --> 00:23:32,940
which then, based on its high concentration, can precipitate out as calcifications.

289
00:23:32,940 --> 00:23:39,940
That's called metastatic calcification, and it's always associated with a high serum calcium or a high serum phosphate.

290
00:23:39,940 --> 00:23:45,940
Now, let me just remind you that the term metastatic calcification doesn't mean that the patient has metastatic cancer.

291
00:23:45,940 --> 00:23:48,940
That's, unfortunately, sometimes confused by students.

292
00:23:48,940 --> 00:23:52,940
Metastatic calcification just means that the serum calcium is high throughout the blood,

293
00:23:52,940 --> 00:23:56,940
and therefore the calcium can deposit throughout all the tissues of the body.

294
00:23:56,940 --> 00:23:59,940
And so it kind of is metastatic.

295
00:23:59,940 --> 00:24:05,940
Now, it so happens that one of the causes of a high serum calcium is metastatic cancer to the bone,

296
00:24:05,940 --> 00:24:09,940
which could then result in metastatic calcification due to the high serum calcium.

297
00:24:09,940 --> 00:24:13,940
However, I really want to make it clear that when we say metastatic calcification,

298
00:24:13,940 --> 00:24:21,940
what we mean is calcification that occurs in multiple tissues due to a high serum or a high phosphate.

299
00:24:21,940 --> 00:24:24,940
All right, so let's keep moving forward.

300
00:24:24,940 --> 00:24:25,940
Fibrinoid necrosis.

301
00:24:25,940 --> 00:24:26,940
This is another type of necrosis.

302
00:24:26,940 --> 00:24:30,940
It refers to necrotic damage of the blood vessel wall.

303
00:24:30,940 --> 00:24:32,940
Necrotic damage to the blood vessel wall.

304
00:24:32,940 --> 00:24:37,940
This damage results in leaking of proteins from the blood vessel into the wall,

305
00:24:37,940 --> 00:24:41,940
which then results in a bright pink staining on histology.

306
00:24:41,940 --> 00:24:43,940
So let me show you a picture.

307
00:24:43,940 --> 00:24:49,940
This is figure 1.10 in your text, and this is a blood vessel highlighted here.

308
00:24:49,940 --> 00:24:50,940
I'm outlining it for you.

309
00:24:50,940 --> 00:24:55,940
And you can clearly see that there is this bright pink staining to the wall of the blood vessel.

310
00:24:55,940 --> 00:24:58,940
This is an example of fibrinoid necrosis.

311
00:24:58,940 --> 00:25:03,940
Let me take you a step back and remind you that this characteristic of two pathologic processes.

312
00:25:03,940 --> 00:25:06,940
The first is malignant hypertension.

313
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:08,940
Why don't we go back.

314
00:25:08,940 --> 00:25:09,940
What is malignant hypertension?

315
00:25:09,940 --> 00:25:14,940
Well, hypertension or high blood pressure can be divided into benign and malignant.

316
00:25:14,940 --> 00:25:24,940
Benign hypertension is a sort of low-grade hypertension that creates long-term progressive chronic damage to organs.

317
00:25:24,940 --> 00:25:30,940
Malignant hypertension is a very high blood pressure that presents with clinical findings such as headache,

318
00:25:30,940 --> 00:25:35,940
renal failure, papilledema, and is a medical emergency.

319
00:25:35,940 --> 00:25:42,940
One of the consequences of malignant hypertension is that the blood vessel wall can actually necrose due to the high pressure.

320
00:25:42,940 --> 00:25:47,940
And that's an example, I should say, of fibrinoid necrosis.

321
00:25:47,940 --> 00:25:49,940
So very important to remember.

322
00:25:49,940 --> 00:25:53,940
Another example of fibrinoid necrosis is vasculitis.

323
00:25:53,940 --> 00:25:59,940
So in a subset of vasculitis, the characteristic finding would be fibrinoid necrosis.

324
00:25:59,940 --> 00:26:03,940
And we'll have a chance to discuss that when we do the blood vessel chapter.

325
00:26:03,940 --> 00:26:10,940
Now, I'd like to say one more thing in trying to give you a classic question that might show up on an exam.

326
00:26:10,940 --> 00:26:20,940
Let's just ask you, in what circumstance would a 30-year-old woman present with fibrinoid necrosis?

327
00:26:20,940 --> 00:26:26,940
Now, the reason I ask this question is just sort of to jog your memory and just stick a little point into your brain.

328
00:26:26,940 --> 00:26:30,940
And that is that, you know, a 30-year-old woman would not normally have malignant hypertension.

329
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:34,940
And the vast majority of 30-year-old women would not have vasculitis.

330
00:26:34,940 --> 00:26:40,940
But there is one circumstance in which a woman could get very high blood pressure, and that's preeclampsia.

331
00:26:40,940 --> 00:26:43,940
Now, what happens in preeclampsia?

332
00:26:43,940 --> 00:26:52,940
And preeclampsia usually presents in the third trimester with an elevated blood pressure, often with proteinuria.

333
00:26:52,940 --> 00:26:59,940
And the consequence of preeclampsia is fibrinoid necrosis.

334
00:26:59,940 --> 00:27:02,940
Of what organ? Of the placenta.

335
00:27:02,940 --> 00:27:11,940
So, fibrinoid necrosis of the placenta, of the placental blood vessels, is a consequence of preeclampsia.

336
00:27:11,940 --> 00:27:16,940
And, believe me, that's a very commonly tested principle.

337
00:27:16,940 --> 00:27:21,940
Okay, so that really ends our discussion of necrosis.

338
00:27:21,940 --> 00:27:25,940
And now, we're actually going to move on to apoptosis.

339
00:27:25,940 --> 00:27:30,940
Remember that I told you the way I sort of think about apoptosis is it's cellular suicide.

340
00:27:30,940 --> 00:27:32,940
It is energy-dependent.

341
00:27:32,940 --> 00:27:40,940
The cell decides that it wants to purposely use energy to kill itself, and it's genetically programmed.

342
00:27:40,940 --> 00:27:45,940
It runs through a genetic program in order to destroy itself.

343
00:27:45,940 --> 00:27:53,940
Usually, it involves single cells, or small groups of cells, as opposed to necrosis, which involves a large group of cells.

344
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:56,940
A few classic examples to be familiar with.

345
00:27:56,940 --> 00:28:01,940
The endometrium, when it sheds, actually sheds via apoptosis during the menstrual cycle.

346
00:28:01,940 --> 00:28:08,940
During embryogenesis, cells are often removed, and that removal of cells occurs via apoptosis.

347
00:28:08,940 --> 00:28:15,940
Now, for example, the spaces that are present between your fingers and toes, that occurs by removal of tissue via apoptosis.

348
00:28:15,940 --> 00:28:21,940
And sometimes, you'll see patients with something called syndactyly, where there are two toes stuck together.

349
00:28:21,940 --> 00:28:27,940
And that is because apoptosis did not occur during embryogenesis in that particular tissue.

350
00:28:27,940 --> 00:28:36,940
And a third classic example of apoptosis would be the killing of virally infected cells by CD8-positive T cells.

351
00:28:36,940 --> 00:28:45,940
Now, recall that when virus infects a cell, the proteins of that virus are actually produced by the genetic machinery of the cell,

352
00:28:45,940 --> 00:28:50,940
and therefore, because these are endogenous proteins, they'll be expressed on MHC class 1.

353
00:28:50,940 --> 00:28:57,940
MHC class 1 is present on all nucleated cells and platelets, and it expresses endogenous protein.

354
00:28:57,940 --> 00:29:05,940
MHC class 1, once it expresses these viral proteins, will be recognized by CD8-positive T cells.

355
00:29:05,940 --> 00:29:11,940
And the CD8-positive T cells, the means by which they kill virally infected cells, is actually apoptosis.

356
00:29:11,940 --> 00:29:14,940
So that becomes relatively high yield.

357
00:29:14,940 --> 00:29:17,940
The morphology of apoptosis.

358
00:29:17,940 --> 00:29:21,940
Let's go back and remind ourselves of the meaning of the word apoptosis.

359
00:29:21,940 --> 00:29:23,940
Apoptosis means the falling of leaves.

360
00:29:23,940 --> 00:29:26,940
It's a Greek term that means the falling of leaves.

361
00:29:26,940 --> 00:29:31,940
And so the idea is that let's pretend that this is the cell, and the cell is going to die.

362
00:29:31,940 --> 00:29:37,940
Well, the way by which the cell dies is that this cell eventually shrinks.

363
00:29:37,940 --> 00:29:40,940
The nucleus in the cell shrinks.

364
00:29:40,940 --> 00:29:49,940
And then eventually, little pieces of the cell sort of butt off and fall off the cell as if leaves are falling from a tree.

365
00:29:49,940 --> 00:29:59,940
And so the word apoptosis means the falling of leaves, and when the cell eventually dies and breaks up, you see the falling of pieces, falling off of pieces of the cell.

366
00:29:59,940 --> 00:30:01,940
So that's the name apoptosis.

367
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:07,940
Now, from this image that I just drew, you get the idea that a couple things have to happen.

368
00:30:07,940 --> 00:30:14,940
First of all, the cell has to shrink, and when the cell shrinks, it becomes very pink because the cytoplasm gets concentrated within that cell.

369
00:30:14,940 --> 00:30:20,940
The second is that the nucleus has to condense or shrink, and then it has to fragment.

370
00:30:20,940 --> 00:30:30,940
And the third is that the apoptotic bodies will fall off from the cell, and it's important to remember that these apoptotic bodies are removed by macrophages.

371
00:30:30,940 --> 00:30:33,940
Apoptotic bodies are removed by macrophages.

372
00:30:33,940 --> 00:30:38,940
There is no inflammation, no acute inflammation that follows apoptosis.

373
00:30:38,940 --> 00:30:42,940
And this is important because it's a contrasting point against necrosis.

374
00:30:42,940 --> 00:30:49,940
Remember that necrosis is followed by acute inflammation, whereas apoptosis, which is not followed by inflammation.

375
00:30:49,940 --> 00:30:52,940
Here's a classic image, figure 1.1 in your text.

376
00:30:52,940 --> 00:30:56,940
And you can see here that this, I'm boxing out here, the apoptotic cell.

377
00:30:56,940 --> 00:31:06,940
So notice, if you compare it to its neighbors, its cytoplasm, which is this stuff here, its cytoplasm is pink because it's getting concentrated as the cell shrinks,

378
00:31:06,940 --> 00:31:14,940
and the nucleus here is a little smaller than the rest of the neighboring cells because the nucleus is also shrinking.

379
00:31:14,940 --> 00:31:19,940
Once you understand the morphology, you can then understand how apoptosis is mediated.

380
00:31:19,940 --> 00:31:26,940
Now, the key mediator of apoptosis is something called a caspase, and that's high yield.

381
00:31:26,940 --> 00:31:30,940
So caspases are the enzymes that mediate apoptosis.

382
00:31:30,940 --> 00:31:31,940
And what do they do?

383
00:31:31,940 --> 00:31:32,940
They do a couple things.

384
00:31:32,940 --> 00:31:34,940
Number one, they activate proteases.

385
00:31:34,940 --> 00:31:35,940
And why do they activate proteases?

386
00:31:35,940 --> 00:31:38,940
Because the cytoskeleton needs to be broken down.

387
00:31:38,940 --> 00:31:46,940
Let's go back and remind ourselves that a cell maintains its normal size and shape by the presence of a skeleton, which is called a cytoskeleton.

388
00:31:46,940 --> 00:31:49,940
That cytoskeleton is predominantly composed of protein.

389
00:31:49,940 --> 00:31:56,940
If we want to take a cell from this size and we want to bring it down to this size, i.e., if we want to shrink it, we've got to trash that cytoskeleton.

390
00:31:56,940 --> 00:31:57,940
And who's going to do that?

391
00:31:57,940 --> 00:31:58,940
The proteases.

392
00:31:58,940 --> 00:31:59,940
Alright.

393
00:31:59,940 --> 00:32:06,940
In the same way, if this is the cell and the cell contains this nucleus and the cell is shrinking and the nucleus is going to shrink,

394
00:32:06,940 --> 00:32:10,940
then you also need to be able to crunch down that nucleus and break it down.

395
00:32:10,940 --> 00:32:19,940
And so endonucleases are the mechanism by which the DNA of the cell gets broken down.

396
00:32:19,940 --> 00:32:24,940
So apoptosis, the key mediator, are the caspases.

397
00:32:24,940 --> 00:32:30,940
And the caspases eventually activate the proteases, which allow for shrinking and breaking down of the cytoskeleton,

398
00:32:30,940 --> 00:32:35,940
and endonucleases, which allow for breaking down and shrinking of the DNA.

399
00:32:35,940 --> 00:32:36,940
Okay.

400
00:32:36,940 --> 00:32:38,940
Now, very high yield.

401
00:32:38,940 --> 00:32:44,940
Caspases, which are the key enzymes that sort of mediate apoptosis, are activated by three major pathways.

402
00:32:44,940 --> 00:32:47,940
And each of these pathways is relatively high yield.

403
00:32:47,940 --> 00:32:51,940
Now, the first is called the intrinsic mitochondrial pathway.

404
00:32:51,940 --> 00:33:01,940
And the idea here is that if there is something that is going to result, let's say that there is some force that is going to cause apoptosis.

405
00:33:01,940 --> 00:33:03,940
So what are some things that can cause apoptosis?

406
00:33:03,940 --> 00:33:04,940
Cellular injury.

407
00:33:04,940 --> 00:33:11,940
Do you guys remember from cellular injury, what did I tell you, was the key sign of irreversible damage?

408
00:33:11,940 --> 00:33:13,940
It's an irreversible injury.

409
00:33:13,940 --> 00:33:15,940
The key sign was membrane damage.

410
00:33:15,940 --> 00:33:18,940
And one of those membranes that was going to be damaged was the mitochondria.

411
00:33:18,940 --> 00:33:21,940
And that can result in activation of this pathway.

412
00:33:21,940 --> 00:33:23,940
DNA damage.

413
00:33:23,940 --> 00:33:26,940
When DNA is damaged, you don't want those cells hanging around.

414
00:33:26,940 --> 00:33:28,940
Why don't you want those cells hanging around?

415
00:33:28,940 --> 00:33:32,940
Because you could get cancer from a cell that has damaged DNA.

416
00:33:32,940 --> 00:33:43,940
And so if the DNA damage is severe in a cell, it can actually undergo apoptosis to protect the rest of the organ from the formation of an eventual cancer.

417
00:33:43,940 --> 00:33:45,940
And decreased hormonal stimulation.

418
00:33:45,940 --> 00:33:48,940
For example, the endometrial cycle.

419
00:33:48,940 --> 00:33:54,940
We know that when progesterone comes in, estrogen is removed and progesterone comes in.

420
00:33:54,940 --> 00:33:58,940
Eventually, we get shedding of the endometrium and that occurs via apoptosis.

421
00:33:58,940 --> 00:34:03,940
Now, the examiners like to go after the following principle.

422
00:34:03,940 --> 00:34:11,940
And that is that the mechanism, sort of the key molecule that mediates this intrinsic mitochondrial pathway, is cytochrome C.

423
00:34:11,940 --> 00:34:14,940
All right, let's highlight that with a drawing.

424
00:34:14,940 --> 00:34:16,940
So this is the cell.

425
00:34:16,940 --> 00:34:20,940
And let's pretend that within this cell, we've got this mitochondria.

426
00:34:20,940 --> 00:34:24,940
And we know that within the mitochondria here is cytochrome C.

427
00:34:24,940 --> 00:34:30,940
And so, cytochrome C is very carefully guarded within that mitochondria.

428
00:34:30,940 --> 00:34:33,940
In fact, there's a molecule here called BCL2.

429
00:34:33,940 --> 00:34:40,940
And BCL2's role is to stabilize the mitochondrial membrane so that cytochrome C cannot leak out.

430
00:34:40,940 --> 00:34:53,940
If there is a stimulus that is going to result in apoptosis, for example, let's say that there's DNA damage or there is cellular injury or there is a decrease in hormonal stimulation,

431
00:34:53,940 --> 00:34:56,940
what will happen is that BCL2 will get knocked out.

432
00:34:56,940 --> 00:35:08,940
And when BCL2 gets knocked out, cytochrome C can leak out from the mitochondria into the cytoplasm and can activate the caspases necessary to drive apoptosis further.

433
00:35:08,940 --> 00:35:14,940
So this idea of cytochrome C and BCL2 is a relatively high yield principle.

434
00:35:14,940 --> 00:35:25,940
The next way by which apoptosis can be activated, or in particular the caspases can be activated, is the extrinsic receptor ligand pathway.

435
00:35:25,940 --> 00:35:32,940
And the basic idea here is that something from the outside world binds a receptor on the cell, which then activates apoptosis.

436
00:35:32,940 --> 00:35:41,940
And the classic example, and the one that probably we should focus on, is FAS ligand binding FAS death receptor on the target cell.

437
00:35:41,940 --> 00:35:52,940
Why don't we, the best example I think of this is to, and it requires a little review of immunology, is to go back and remind ourselves that T cells are actually born in the bone marrow.

438
00:35:52,940 --> 00:35:57,940
And once they're produced in the bone marrow, they go to the thymus to be educated.

439
00:35:57,940 --> 00:36:02,940
And when they're educated in the thymus, they actually have to go through two layers of testing.

440
00:36:02,940 --> 00:36:15,940
The first is called positive selection. Positive selection, they get asked the question, do you, i.e. the T cell, have the ability to bind self-antigen plus MHC?

441
00:36:15,940 --> 00:36:25,940
If the T cell has the ability to bind self-antigen plus MHC, it survives positive selection and goes on to the next sort of test.

442
00:36:25,940 --> 00:36:36,940
The next test is called negative selection, and in negative selection, the question is asked, do you, i.e. the T cell, have the ability to bind self-antigen too avidly, too strongly?

443
00:36:36,940 --> 00:36:44,940
And if the T cell binds self-antigen too strongly, the T cell will then be destroyed during the process of negative selection.

444
00:36:44,940 --> 00:36:51,940
You want to get rid of the T cells that are going to bind to self-antigen because you don't want to have autoimmune problems.

445
00:36:51,940 --> 00:37:00,940
And so the mechanism by which the cells die in negative selection is actually by the fast ligand, fast death receptor pathway.

446
00:37:00,940 --> 00:37:02,940
It's apoptosis basically.

447
00:37:02,940 --> 00:37:16,940
And so if the cells too avidly bind self-antigen, then fast ligand is expressed and it hits the death receptor on the T cell and causes the T cell to undergo apoptosis.

448
00:37:16,940 --> 00:37:20,940
And this becomes a very high yield way by which this pathway can be tested.

449
00:37:20,940 --> 00:37:26,940
Another example would be tumor necrosis factor binding tumor necrosis factor receptor on a target cell.

450
00:37:26,940 --> 00:37:39,940
But for me, this really is the high yield, fast ligand, fast death receptor in the context of negative selection of T cells in the thymus.

451
00:37:39,940 --> 00:37:46,940
And the third pathway by which caspases can be activated is the cytotoxic CD8 positive T cell pathway.

452
00:37:46,940 --> 00:37:51,940
Now, recall that CD8 positive T cells, they recognize antigen present on MHC class I.

453
00:37:51,940 --> 00:37:58,940
And when they bind and recognize an antigen on MHC class I, they kill the cell that expresses the antigen.

454
00:37:58,940 --> 00:38:01,940
And the way by which they kill the cell is apoptosis.

455
00:38:01,940 --> 00:38:10,940
So what happens is the CD8 positive T cell, once it recognizes its antigen and gets the appropriate signal, will actually secrete something called a perforin.

456
00:38:10,940 --> 00:38:14,940
The perforin perforates a hole in the membrane of the target cell.

457
00:38:14,940 --> 00:38:22,940
Granzyme, also from the CD8 positive T cell, then enters into the pore or the hole and then activates the caspases.

458
00:38:22,940 --> 00:38:26,940
And you're very well aware of the fact that caspases then mediate apoptosis.

459
00:38:26,940 --> 00:38:33,940
So that's another important pathway by which apoptosis can be activated, i.e. the caspases can be activated.

460
00:38:33,940 --> 00:38:41,940
The Cure of Junction with Joc-Azum in the water.

461
00:38:41,940 --> 00:38:46,940
Remember when you had an antigen on MHC?

462
00:38:46,940 --> 00:38:48,940
The Cure of Izum in the water can be activated.

463
00:38:48,940 --> 00:38:53,940
The Cure of Junction with Joc-Azum in the water can be activated.

464
00:38:53,940 --> 00:38:58,940
The Cure of Junction with Joc-Azum in the water can be activated.

